  "name": "Question 2",
  "problem": "${lim}_{x\\to -\\infty }(-\\frac{7}{{x}^{5}})=$",
  "worked_solution": "First, we use the algebra of limits to pull out the constant factor of $-7:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{lim}_{x\\to -\\infty }(-\\frac{7}{{x}^{5}}) & ={lim}_{x\\to -\\infty }(-7\\cdot \\frac{1}{{x}^{5}}) \\\\  & =-7\\cdot {lim}_{x\\to -\\infty }\\frac{1}{{x}^{5}}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, using the fact that\n\n$$\n{lim}_{x\\to -\\infty }\\frac{1}{{x}^{5}}=0,\n$$\n\nwe can evaluate our limit as follows:\n\n$$\n-7\\cdot {lim}_{x\\to -\\infty }\\frac{1}{{x}^{5}}=-7\\cdot 0=0\n$$",
